          "content": "**OpenSTEM Materials Science & Engineering | Book 02 | Original educational draft | 27 October 2026**\n\nCrystallography concerns how solids arrange matter in ordered structures and how physical measurements can test those structural descriptions. Diffraction is not a camera recording a direct atomic photograph. It is an interference measurement in which detector angles and intensities are transformed into structure claims using geometry, scattering physics, calibration, and models. A useful crystallographer learns to keep observations separate from interpretation. A peak angle is observed; a d-spacing is calculated under wavelength and geometry assumptions; a crystal structure is inferred by fitting many constraints. Treat those as different levels of evidence.\n\nThis book builds from the direct lattice to reciprocal-space constructions, wave scattering, powder and single-crystal diffraction, microstructure-sensitive profile analysis, and research-data integrity. The course is written for physics, chemistry, materials science and engineering undergraduates who have studied vectors, introductory waves, logarithms, unit conversion, and basic probability. Some advanced sections use tensors, Fourier transforms, refinement and regression; those are explained conceptually where a derivation would exceed the core prerequisites.",
