          "content": "1. Translate between periodic coordinates, symmetry operations, planes and reciprocal-space geometry.\n2. Predict ideal peak positions with Bragg's relation and distinguish structural selection rules from measurement limitations.\n3. Evaluate what powder and single-crystal diffraction can, and cannot, establish about composition, phase, texture, domain size and stress.\n4. Explain why profile correction, uncertainty, calibration, data retention and model comparison are essential parts of scientific interpretation.\n5. Package results with transparent metadata, mathematical assumptions and stated limitations for independent scrutiny.\n\n\n# Unit 1: Lattices, cells, and symmetry\n\n**Guiding question:** How do we specify periodic order without confusing a mathematical lattice with an actual arrangement of atoms?\n\nThis unit connects cell geometry and symmetry to an observable experiment. Every example is fictional and intentionally simplified. The sequence is designed so that the first two lessons provide definitions, the middle lessons test consequences, and the fifth lesson confronts ambiguity. Read plots as conditional calculations rather than laboratory observations.",
